{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Predicting Negative Customer Reviews in Brazilian E-Commerce\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook presents the integrated analysis used in the portfolio project. The project compares negative-review prediction at order placement with prediction after delivery, and adds an unsupervised view of order-level risk patterns.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Target:** \`review_bad = 1\` for review scores 1–2 and \`0\` for scores 3–5.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Final sample:** 95,824 delivered orders; negative-review rate approximately 12.8%."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Analytical design\n",
    "\n",
    "The same held-out orders are used for the placement and delivery models. This keeps the comparison focused on information availability rather than differences in the test sample. Delivery outcomes are excluded from the placement model to avoid look-ahead leakage."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "comparison = pd.read_csv('../outputs/placement_vs_delivery.csv')\n",
    "comparison"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Order segmentation\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means was used to create four order personas from order value, freight, product weight, delivery duration, delivery performance relative to promise, installments and number of items. Review outcome was not used to fit the clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "The four-cluster solution is treated as an interpretable segmentation rather than a claim of strong natural separation. The original silhouette score was about 0.21."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "clusters = pd.read_csv('../outputs/cluster_profile.csv')\n",
    "clusters[['cluster','share','bad_review_rate','avg_delivery_time_days','avg_n_items']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Two segments stand out. The **Long Wait** group has a negative-review rate of about 28.9% and is characterized by poor delivery performance. The **Multi-Item Basket** group reaches about 25.9% despite generally arriving early. This suggests that review risk is not explained by lateness alone."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Placement versus delivery\n",
    "\n",
    "The placement model uses information available when the order is created. The delivery model adds observed fulfillment information. This produces a practical trade-off: the earlier model leaves more time to intervene, while the later model has stronger signal."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "comparison.set_index('stage')[['precision','recall','f1','roc_auc']].round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ax = comparison.set_index('stage')[['f1','roc_auc']].plot(kind='bar', figsize=(7,4))\n",
    "ax.set_ylim(0, 1)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Score')\n",
    "ax.set_title('Predictive performance improves after fulfillment information is observed')\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=0)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "F1 increases from 0.318 at placement to 0.484 at delivery, while ROC-AUC increases from 0.677 to 0.768. The placement model retains higher recall, but the delivery model is substantially more precise."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Final-model interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "CatBoost is the final delivery-stage model. Feature importance is used as a predictive diagnostic; it should not be interpreted as a causal effect."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "importance = pd.read_csv('../outputs/catboost_feature_importance.csv')\n",
    "importance.head(10)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "top = importance.head(10).sort_values('importance')\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))\n",
    "plt.barh(top['feature'], top['importance'])\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Feature importance')\n",
    "plt.title('At-delivery CatBoost: leading predictive features')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The strongest features are \`late_days\`, \`delivery_vs_estimate_days\`, \`n_items\` and \`delivery_time_days\`. This is consistent with the segmentation results: fulfillment problems matter, but order complexity also carries information."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Business interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "A two-stage workflow is more useful than treating the models as substitutes. At placement, the model can support inexpensive monitoring or proactive communication. After fulfillment, the stronger delivery-stage model can prioritize service-recovery attention.\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis does not estimate the causal effect of an intervention. It identifies risk patterns and prediction opportunities that would need to be tested operationally."
   ]
  }
 ],
